1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:04,729
Every Jira admin gets this ticket: why can't Emma see
PAY-3?

2
00:00:04,729 --> 00:00:10,058
Tracing schemes, roles, groups and security levels by hand
takes hours.

3
00:00:10,058 --> 00:00:13,334
Config Doctor answers in seconds, and proves it.

4
00:00:17,650 --> 00:00:23,508
In Rovo, I pick the Config Doctor agent and ask: why can't
Emma Paris see PAY-3?

5
00:00:23,508 --> 00:00:33,166
The answer comes from a deterministic Forge action that
reads the permission scheme, roles, groups and issue
security, not from the model's guesswork.

6
00:00:33,166 --> 00:00:36,350
Verdict: blocked by the issue security level.

7
00:00:36,350 --> 00:00:49,495
The evidence chain shows Emma passes the permission
scheme, through group cd-contractors-2024 in role CD
Developers, but the level Internal only admits only
cd-staff-hq.

8
00:00:49,495 --> 00:00:55,074
The fix, adding her to that group, is simulated first,
with its blast radius.

9
00:00:55,074 --> 00:00:57,482
And Jira's own permission check agrees.

10
00:00:58,583 --> 00:01:02,244
Next: who can see PAY-3, and why?

11
00:01:02,244 --> 00:01:09,928
Three people, Alana, Kevin and the reporter, each with the
exact access path through cd-staff-hq.

12
00:01:09,928 --> 00:01:15,336
The CD Developers role can browse the project, but the
security level shuts it out.

13
00:01:15,336 --> 00:01:19,375
Every viewer is checked against Jira's own list, and it
agrees.

14
00:01:20,650 --> 00:01:28,337
Every call lands in the audit log on the admin page: who
asked, which action, the result, and how long it took.

15
00:01:28,337 --> 00:01:33,758
Here is yesterday's run of the same question about Emma:
the same verdict, word for word.

16
00:01:33,758 --> 00:01:38,705
Across our scripted test runs, thirty out of thirty
answers were identical.

17
00:01:45,117 --> 00:01:47,501
How healthy is the configuration?

18
00:01:47,501 --> 00:01:57,211
Forty-two out of a hundred, from the latest scan of all
twenty-five projects, with an explainable breakdown and
ranked findings, each with evidence.

19
00:01:57,211 --> 00:02:03,185
Show the duplicate fields: five sets, each with field IDs
and a recommendation.

20
00:02:03,185 --> 00:02:07,382
Now, create a cleanup plan in PAY for the top three
findings.

21
00:02:07,382 --> 00:02:14,027
Config Doctor previews exactly what it would create, one
epic and three tasks, and asks me to confirm.

22
00:02:14,027 --> 00:02:15,106
I say yes.

23
00:02:15,106 --> 00:02:20,172
Rovo then shows its own confirmation card, bound to a
single-use token.

24
00:02:20,172 --> 00:02:27,909
Only after that click does it write: epic PAY-12 and three
tasks, labelled and recorded in the audit log.

25
00:02:42,617 --> 00:02:45,106
Before changing anything, simulate it.

26
00:02:45,106 --> 00:02:50,080
What happens if I remove group cd-contractors-2024?

27
00:02:50,080 --> 00:02:51,290
Medium risk.

28
00:02:51,290 --> 00:02:58,461
Max Taylor and Ryan Lee lose browse, create, edit and
transition in both PAY and VEND.

29
00:02:58,461 --> 00:03:03,001
Emma Paris loses VEND, but keeps PAY through cd-dev-vn.

30
00:03:03,001 --> 00:03:07,001
It also lists every role and scheme that references the
group.

31
00:03:07,001 --> 00:03:11,001
A read-only simulation: nothing in Jira is changed.

32
00:03:12,117 --> 00:03:14,356
Judges don't need our seed scripts.

33
00:03:14,356 --> 00:03:25,290
On the admin page, the opt-in Demo Lab previews every
object it will create, then builds a small, labelled
scenario on your own site, using only your account.

34
00:03:25,290 --> 00:03:28,646
Try these: why can't I see CDLAB-2?

35
00:03:28,646 --> 00:03:34,277
Blocked by issue security, Jira agrees, and the fix shows
its blast radius.

36
00:03:34,277 --> 00:03:38,001
Remove demo lab later deletes exactly what it recorded.

37
00:03:39,150 --> 00:03:47,718
The admin page works even without Rovo: weekly scans and a
